There are six Cases in Latin:—
⟦b:Nominative⟧,  Case of Subject;
⟦b:Genitive⟧,  Objective with ⟦i:of⟧, or Possessive;
⟦b:Dative⟧,  Objective with ⟦i:to⟧ or ⟦i:for⟧;
⟦b:Accusative⟧,  Case of Direct Object;
⟦b:Vocative⟧,  Case of Address;
⟦b:Ablative⟧,  Objective with ⟦i:by⟧, ⟦i:from⟧, ⟦i:in⟧, ⟦i:with⟧.
⟦/⟧
1. LOCATIVE. Vestiges of another case, the ⟦b:Locative⟧ (denoting place where), occur in names of towns and in a few other words.
2. OBLIQUE CASES. The Genitive, Dative, Accusative, and Ablative are called ⟦b:Oblique Cases⟧.
3. STEM AND CASE-ENDINGS. The different cases are formed by appending certain ⟦b:case-endings⟧ to a fundamental part called the ⟦b:Stem⟧. Thus, ⟦i:portam⟧ (Accusative Singular) is formed by adding the case-ending ⟦b:-m⟧ to the stem ⟦b:porta-⟧. But in most cases the final vowel of the stem has coalesced so closely with the actual case-ending that the latter has become more or less obscured. The ⟦i:apparent case-ending⟧ thus resulting is called a ⟦b:termination⟧.

The Genitive is used with many Adjectives ⟦i:to limit the extent of their application⟧. Thus:—
1. With adjectives signifying ⟦i:desire⟧, ⟦i:knowledge⟧, ⟦i:familiarity⟧, ⟦i:memory⟧, ⟦i:participation⟧, ⟦i:power⟧, ⟦i:fullness⟧, and their opposites; as,—
⟦b:studiōsus discendī⟧, ⟦i:desirous of learning⟧;
⟦b:perītus bellī⟧, ⟦i:skilled in war⟧;
⟦b:īnsuētus labōris⟧, ⟦i:unused to toil⟧;
⟦b:immemor mandātī tuī⟧, ⟦i:unmindful of your commission⟧;
⟦b:plēna perīculōrum est vīta⟧, ⟦i:life is full of dangers⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. Some participles used adjectively also take the Genitive; as,—
⟦b:diligēns vēritātis⟧, ⟦i:fond of truth⟧;
⟦b:amāns patriae⟧, ⟦i:devoted to one's country⟧.
2. Sometimes with ⟦b:proprius⟧ and ⟦b:commūnis;⟧ as,—
⟦b:virī propria est fortitūdō⟧, ⟦i:bravery is characteristic of a man⟧.
⟦b:memoria est commūnis omnium artium⟧, ⟦i:memory is common to all professions⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. ⟦b:proprius⟧ and ⟦b:commūnis⟧ are also construed with the Dative.
3. With ⟦b:similis⟧ the Genitive is the commoner construction in Cicero, when the reference is to living objects; as,—
⟦b:fīlius patris simillimus est⟧, ⟦i:the son is exactly like his father⟧;
⟦b:meī similis⟧, ⟦i:like me⟧; ⟦b:vestrī similis⟧, ⟦i:like you⟧.
When the reference is to things, both Genitive and Dative occur; as,—
⟦b:mors somnō⟧ (or ⟦b:somnī⟧) ⟦b:similis est⟧, ⟦i:death is like sleep⟧.
4. In the poets and later prose writers the use of the Genitive with Adjectives is extended far beyond earlier limits; as, ⟦b:atrōx animī⟧, ⟦i:fierce of temper⟧; ⟦b:incertus cōnsilī⟧, ⟦i:undecided in purpose⟧.

1. PLURAL ADJECTIVES USED SUBSTANTIVELY. Adjectives are quite freely used as Substantives in the Plural. The Masculine denotes persons; the Neuter denotes things; as,—
⟦b:doctī⟧, ⟦i:scholars⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:parva⟧, ⟦i:small things⟧;
⟦b:malī⟧, ⟦i:the wicked⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:magna⟧, ⟦i:great things⟧;
⟦b:Graecī⟧, ⟦i:the Greeks⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:ūtilia⟧, ⟦i:useful things⟧;
⟦.⟧  ⟦b:nostrī⟧, ⟦i:our men⟧.
⟦/⟧
2. Neuter Plural Adjectives thus used are confined mainly to the Nominative and Accusative cases. Such forms as ⟦b:magnōrum⟧, ⟦b:omnium⟧; ⟦b:magnīs⟧, ⟦b:omnibus⟧, would ordinarily lead to ambiguity; yet where there is no ambiguity, they sometimes occur; as,—
⟦b:parvīs compōnere magna⟧, ⟦i:to compare great things with small⟧
Otherwise the Latin says: ⟦b:magnārum rērum⟧, ⟦b:magnīs rēbus⟧, ⟦i:etc.⟧
SINGULAR ADJECTIVES USED SUBSTANTIVELY. Adjectives are less freely used as Substantives in the Singular than in the Plural.
1. Masculine Adjectives occur only occasionally in this use; as,—
⟦b:probus invidet nēminī⟧, ⟦i:the honest man envies nobody⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. Usually ⟦b:vir⟧, ⟦b:homō⟧, or some similar word is employed; as,—
⟦b:homō doctus⟧, ⟦i:a scholar⟧;
⟦b:vir Rōmānus⟧, ⟦i:a Roman⟧.
⟦i:b⟧. But when limited by a pronoun any adjective may be so used; as,—
⟦b:hīc doctus⟧, ⟦i:this scholar⟧;
⟦b:doctus quīdam⟧, ⟦i:a certain scholar⟧.
2. Neuters are likewise infrequent; as,—
⟦b:vērum⟧, ⟦i:truth⟧;
⟦b:jūstum⟧, ⟦i:justice⟧;
⟦b:honestum⟧, ⟦i:virtue⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. This substantive use of Neuter Singulars is commonest in the construction of the Genitive of the Whole, and after Prepositions; as,—
⟦b:aliquid vērī⟧, ⟦i:something true⟧;
⟦b:nihil novī⟧, ⟦i:nothing new⟧;
⟦b:in mediō⟧, ⟦i:in the midst⟧.
From Adjectives which, like the above, occasionally admit the substantive use, must be carefully distinguished certain others which have become nouns; as,—
⟦b:adversārius⟧, ⟦i:opponent⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:hīberna⟧, ⟦i:winter quarters⟧;
⟦b:aequālis⟧, ⟦i:contemporary⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:propinquus⟧, ⟦i:relative⟧;
⟦b:amīcus⟧, ⟦i:friend⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:socius⟧, ⟦i:partner⟧;
⟦b:cognātus⟧, ⟦i:kinsman⟧;  ⟦.⟧  ⟦b:sodālis⟧, ⟦i:comrade⟧;
⟦.⟧  ⟦b:vīcīnus⟧, ⟦i:neighbor⟧; ⟦i:etc.⟧
⟦/⟧

The Latin often uses an Adjective where the English idiom employs an Adverb or an adverbial phrase; as,—
⟦b:senātus frequēns convēnit⟧, ⟦i:the senate assembled in great numbers⟧;
⟦b:fuit assiduus mēcum⟧, ⟦i:he was constantly with me⟧.

1. The Latin has no special reciprocal pronoun ('each other'), but expresses the reciprocal notion by the phrases: ⟦b:inter nōs⟧, ⟦b:inter vōs⟧, ⟦b:inter sē⟧; as,—
⟦b:Belgae obsidēs inter sē dedērunt⟧, ⟦i:the Belgae gave each other hostages⟧ (lit. ⟦i:among themselves⟧);
⟦b:amāmus inter nōs⟧, ⟦i:we love each other⟧;
⟦b:Gallī inter sē cohortātī sunt⟧, ⟦i:the Gauls exhorted each other⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. Note that the Object is not expressed in sentences of this type.
Hīc, Ille, Iste.

The Indicative is used for the ⟦i:statement of facts⟧, ⟦i:the supposition of facts⟧, or ⟦i:inquiry after facts⟧.
1. Note the following idiomatic uses:—
⟦i:a⟧) With ⟦b:possum⟧; as,—
⟦b:possum multa dīcere⟧, ⟦i:I might say much⟧;
⟦b:poteram multa dīcere⟧, ⟦i:I might have said much⟧ (§ 270, 2).
⟦i:b⟧) In such expressions as ⟦b:longum est⟧, ⟦b:aequum est⟧, ⟦b:melius est⟧, ⟦b:difficile est⟧, ⟦b:ūtilius est⟧, and some others; as,—
⟦b:longum est ea dīcere⟧, ⟦i:it would be tedious to tell that⟧;
⟦b:difficile est omnia persequī⟧, ⟦i:it would be difficult to enumerate everything⟧.
The Subjunctive in Independent Sentences.
The Subjunctive is used in Independent Sentences to express something—
⟦b:1. As willed—Volitive Subjunctive⟧;
⟦b:2. As desired—Optative Subjunctive⟧;
⟦b:3. Conceived of as possible—Potential Subjunctive⟧.

The Hortatory Subjunctive expresses ⟦i:an exhortation⟧. This use is confined to the first person plural of the Present. The negative is ⟦b:nē⟧. Thus:—
⟦b:eāmus⟧, ⟦i:let us go⟧;
⟦b:amēmus patriam⟧, ⟦i:let us love our country⟧;
⟦b:nē dēspērēmus⟧, ⟦i:let us not despair⟧.

The Jussive Subjunctive expresses a ⟦i:command⟧. The Jussive stands regularly in the Present Tense, and is used—
1. Most frequently in the third singular and the third plural; as,—
⟦b:dīcat⟧, ⟦i:let him tell⟧;
⟦b:dīcant⟧, ⟦i:let them tell⟧;
⟦b:quārē sēcēdant improbī⟧, ⟦i:wherefore let the wicked depart!⟧
2. Less frequently in the second person, often with indefinite force; as,—
⟦b:istō bonō ūtāre⟧, ⟦i:use that advantage⟧;
⟦b:modestē vīvās⟧, ⟦i:live temperately⟧.

The Subjunctive is used to indicate something ⟦i:as granted or conceded for the sake of argument⟧. The Present is used for present time, the Perfect regularly for past. The negative is ⟦b:nē⟧. Thus:—
⟦b:sit hōc vērum⟧, ⟦i:I grant that this is true⟧ (lit. ⟦i:let this be true⟧);
⟦b:nē sint in senectūte vīrēs⟧, ⟦i:I grant there is not strength in old age⟧;
⟦b:fuerit malus cīvis aliīs; tibi quandō esse coepit⟧, ⟦i:I grant that he was a bad citizen to others; when did he begin to be so toward you?⟧

The Potential Subjunctive expresses ⟦i:a possibility⟧. The negative is ⟦b:nōn⟧. The following uses are to be noted:—
1. ⟦b:The 'May' Potential.⟧—The Potential Subjunctive may designate ⟦i:a mere possibility⟧ (English auxiliary ⟦i:may⟧). Both Present and Perfect occur, and without appreciable difference of meaning. Thus:—
⟦b:dīcat aliquis⟧, ⟦i:some one may say⟧;
⟦b:dīxerit aliquis⟧, ⟦i:some one may say⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. This construction is by no means frequent, and is confined mainly to a few phrases like those given as examples.
2. ⟦b:'Should'-'Would' Potential.⟧—The Potential Subjunctive may represent something as ⟦i:depending upon a condition expressed or understood⟧ (English auxiliary ⟦i:should⟧, ⟦i:would⟧). Both Present and Perfect occur, and without appreciable difference of meaning. Thus:—
⟦b:fortūnam citius reperiās quam retineās⟧, ⟦i:one would more quickly find Fortune than keep it⟧ (⟦i:i.e.⟧ if one should make the trial);
⟦b:crēdiderim⟧, ⟦i:I should believe⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. Here belongs the use of ⟦b:velim⟧, ⟦b:mālim⟧, ⟦b:nōlim⟧, as softened forms of statement for ⟦b:volō⟧, ⟦b:mālō⟧, ⟦b:nōlō⟧. Thus:—
⟦b:velim mihi ignōscās⟧, ⟦i:I wish you would forgive me⟧;
⟦b:nōlim putēs mē jocārī⟧, ⟦i:I don't want you to think I'm joking⟧.
⟦i:b⟧. When the condition is expressed, we get one of the regular types of Conditional Sentences (see § 303); as,—
⟦b:diēs dēficiat, sī cōner ēnumerāre causās⟧, ⟦i:time would fail if I should attempt to enumerate the reasons.⟧
3. ⟦b:'Can'-'Could' Potential⟧.—In the Present and Imperfect the Potential occurs in the second person singular (with ⟦i:indefinite⟧ force; § 356, 3) of a few verbs of ⟦i:perceiving⟧, ⟦i:seeing⟧, ⟦i:thinking⟧, and the like; as,—
⟦b:videās⟧, ⟦b:cernās⟧, ⟦i:one can see⟧, ⟦i:one can perceive⟧;
⟦b:crēderēs⟧, ⟦i:one could believe⟧;
⟦b:vidērēs⟧, ⟦b:cernerēs⟧, ⟦i:one could see⟧, ⟦i:perceive⟧;
⟦b:putārēs⟧, ⟦i:one could imagine⟧.
4. The Imperfect and Pluperfect in the Apodosis of conditional sentences of the contrary-to-fact type (see § 304) are also Potential in character. By omission of the Protasis, such an Apodosis sometimes stands alone, particularly ⟦b:vellem⟧, ⟦b:nōllem⟧, ⟦b:māllem⟧; as,—
⟦b:vellem id quidem⟧, ⟦i:I should wish that⟧ (⟦i:i.e.⟧ were I bold enough).
The Imperative.
The Imperative is used in ⟦i:commands⟧, ⟦i:admonitions⟧ and ⟦i:entreaties⟧ (negative ⟦b:nē⟧), as,—
⟦b:ēgredere ex urbe⟧, ⟦i:depart from the city⟧;
⟦b:mihi ignōsce⟧, ⟦i:pardon me⟧;
⟦b:valē⟧, ⟦i:farewell⟧.
1. The Present is the tense of the Imperative most commonly used, but the Future is employed—
⟦i:a⟧) Where there is a distinct reference to future time, especially in the apodosis of conditional sentences; as,—
⟦b:rem vōbīs prōpōnam; vōs eam penditōte⟧, ⟦i:I will lay the matter before you; do you (then) consider it⟧;
⟦b:sī bene disputābit, tribuitō litterīs Graecis⟧, ⟦i:if he shall speak well, attribute it to Greek literature.⟧
⟦i:b⟧) In laws, treaties, wills, maxims, ⟦i:etc.⟧; as,—
⟦b:cōnsulēs summum jūs habentō⟧, ⟦i:the consuls shall have supreme power⟧;
⟦b:hominem mortuom in urbe nē sepelītō⟧, ⟦i:no one shall bury a dead body in the city⟧;
⟦b:amīcitia rēgī Antiochō cum populō Rōmānō hīs legibus et condiciōnibus estō⟧, ⟦i:let there be friendship between Antiochus and the Roman people on the following terms and conditions⟧;
⟦b:quārtae estō partis Mārcus hērēs⟧, ⟦i:let Marcus be heir to a fourth (of the property⟧);
⟦b:ignōscitō saepe alterī, numquam tibi⟧, ⟦i:forgive your neighbor often, yourself never⟧.
2. Except with the Future Imperative the negative is not used in classical prose. Prohibitions are regularly expressed in other ways. See § 276, ⟦i:b⟧.
3. Questions in the Indicative introduced by ⟦b:quīn⟧ (⟦i:why not?⟧) are often equivalent to an Imperative or to the Hortatory Subjunctive; as,—
⟦b:quīn abīs⟧, ⟦i:go away!⟧ (lit. ⟦i:why don't you go away?⟧);
⟦b:quīn vōcem continētis⟧, ⟦i:keep still!⟧ (lit. ⟦i:why don't you stop your voices?⟧);
⟦b:quīn equōs cōnscendimus⟧, ⟦i:let us mount our horses⟧ (lit. ⟦i:why do we not mount our horses?⟧)
Clauses of Purpose.

The Subjunctive is often used in subordinate clauses whose indirect character is ⟦i:merely implied by the context⟧; as,—
⟦b:dēmōnstrābantur mihi praetereā, quae Sōcratēs dē immortālitāte animōrum disseruisset⟧, ⟦i:there were explained to me besides, the arguments which Socrates had set forth concerning the immortality of the soul⟧ (⟦i:i.e.⟧ the arguments which, it was said, Socrates had set forth);
⟦b:Paetus omnēs librōs quōs pater suus relīquisset mihi dōnāvit⟧, ⟦i:Paetus gave me all the books which (as he said) his father had left⟧.

1. Subordinate clauses dependent upon the Subjunctive are frequently attracted into the same mood especially when they do not express a fact, but constitute ⟦i:an essential part of one complex idea⟧; as,—
⟦b:nēmō avārus adhūc inventus est, cui, quod habēret, esset satis⟧, ⟦i:no miser has yet been found who was satisfed with what he had⟧;
⟦b:cum dīversās causās afferrent, dum fōrmam suī quisque et animī et ingeniī redderent⟧, ⟦i:as they brought forward different arguments, while each mirrored his own individual type of mind and natural bent⟧;
⟦b:quod ego fatear, pudeat?⟧ ⟦i:should I be ashamed of a thing which I admit?⟧
2. Similarly a subordinate clause dependent upon an Infinitive is put in the Subjunctive when the two form one closely united whole; as,—
⟦b:mōs est Athēnīs quotannīs in cōntiōne laudārī eōs quī sint in proeliīs interfectī⟧, ⟦i:it is the custom at Athens every year for those to be publicly eulogized who have been killed in battle⟧. (Here the notion of 'praising those who fell in battle' forms an inseparable whole.)

1. Where a distinct reference to several persons or things is involved, the Latin is frequently ⟦i:much more exact in the use of the Plural⟧ than is the English; as,—
⟦b:domōs eunt⟧, ⟦i:they go home (i.e. to their homes⟧);
⟦b:Germānī corpora cūrant⟧, ⟦i:the Germans care for the body⟧;
⟦b:animōs mīlitum recreat⟧, ⟦i:he renews the courage of the soldiers⟧;
⟦b:diēs noctēsque timēre⟧, ⟦i:to be in a state of fear day and night⟧.
2. In case of Neuter Pronouns and Adjectives used substantively, the Latin often employs the Plural where the English uses the Singular; as,—
⟦b:omnia sunt perdīta⟧, ⟦i:everything is lost⟧;
⟦b:quae cum ita sint⟧, ⟦i:since this is so⟧;
⟦b:haec omnibus pervulgāta sunt⟧, ⟦i:this is very well known to all⟧.
3. The Latin is usually ⟦i:more concrete⟧ than the English, and especially ⟦i:less bold in the personification⟧ of abstract qualities. Thus:—
⟦b:ā puerō, ā puerīs⟧, ⟦i:from boyhood⟧;
⟦b:Sullā dictātōre⟧, ⟦i:in Sulla's dictatorship⟧;
⟦b:mē duce⟧, ⟦i:under my leadership⟧;
⟦b:Rōmānī cum Carthāginiēnsibus pācem fēcērunt⟧ = ⟦i:Rome made peace with Carthage⟧;
⟦b:liber doctrīnae plēnus⟧ = ⟦i:a learned book⟧;
⟦b:prūdentiā Themistoclīs Graecia servāta est⟧ = ⟦i:Themistocles's foresight saved Greece⟧.
4. The Nouns of Agency in ⟦b:-tor⟧ and ⟦b:-sor⟧ (see § 147, 1) denote a ⟦i:permanent⟧ or ⟦i:characteristic activity⟧; as,—
⟦b:accūsātōrēs⟧, ⟦i:(professional) accusers⟧;
⟦b:ōrātōrēs⟧, ⟦i:pleaders⟧;
⟦b:cantōrēs⟧, ⟦i:singers⟧;
⟦b:Arminius, Germāniae līberātor⟧, ⟦i:Arminius, liberator of Germany⟧.
⟦i:a⟧. To denote single instances of an action, other expressions are commonly employed; as,—
⟦b:Numa, quī Rōmulō successit⟧, ⟦i:Numa, successor of Romulus⟧;
⟦b:quī mea legunt⟧, ⟦i:my readers⟧;
⟦b:quī mē audiunt⟧, ⟦i:my auditors⟧.
5. The Latin avoids the use of prepositional phrases as modifiers of a Noun. In English we say: '⟦i:The war against Carthage⟧'; '⟦i:a journey through Gaul⟧'; '⟦i:cities on the sea⟧'; '⟦i:the book in my hands⟧'; '⟦i:the fight at Salamis⟧'; ⟦i:etc.⟧ The Latin in such cases usually employs another mode of expression. Thus:—
⟦i:a⟧) A Genitive; as,—
⟦b:dolor injūriārum⟧, ⟦i:resentment at injuries⟧.
⟦i:b⟧) An Adjective; as,—
⟦b:urbēs maritimae⟧, ⟦i:cities on the sea⟧;
⟦b:pugna Salamīnia⟧, ⟦i:the fight at Salamis⟧.
⟦i:c⟧) A Participle; as,—
⟦b:pugna ad Cannās facta⟧, ⟦i:the battle at Cannae⟧.
⟦i:d⟧) A Relative clause; as,—
⟦b:liber quī in meīs manibus est⟧, ⟦i:the book in my hands⟧.
NOTE.—Yet within certain limits the Latin does employ Prepositional phrases as Noun modifiers. This is particularly frequent when the governing noun is derived from a verb. The following are typical examples:—
⟦b:trānsitus in Britanniam⟧, ⟦i:the passage to Britain⟧;
⟦b:excessus ē vītā⟧, ⟦i:departure from life⟧;
⟦b:odium ergā Rōmānōs⟧, ⟦i:hatred of the Romans⟧;
⟦b:liber dē senectūte⟧, ⟦i:the book on old age⟧;
⟦b:amor in patriam⟧, ⟦i:love for one's country⟧.

1. ⟦b:Special Latin Equivalents for English Adjectives⟧ are—
⟦i:a⟧) A Genitive; as,—
⟦b:virtūtēs animī⟧ = ⟦i:moral virtues⟧;
⟦b:dolōrēs corporis⟧ = ⟦i:bodily ills⟧.
⟦i:b⟧) An Abstract Noun; as,—
⟦b:novitās reī⟧ = ⟦i:the strange circumstance⟧;
⟦b:asperitās viārum⟧ = ⟦i:rough roads⟧.
⟦i:c⟧) Hendiadys (see § 374, 4); as,—
⟦b:ratiō et ōrdō⟧ = ⟦i:systematic order⟧;
⟦b:ārdor et impetus⟧ = ⟦i:eager onset⟧.
⟦i:d⟧) Sometimes an Adverb; as,—
⟦b:omnēs circā populī⟧, ⟦i:all the surrounding tribes⟧;
⟦b:suōs semper hostēs⟧, ⟦i:their perpetual foes⟧.
2. Often a Latin Noun is equivalent to an English Noun modified by an Adjective; as,—
⟦b:doctrīna⟧, ⟦i:theoretical knowledge⟧;
⟦b:prūdentia⟧, ⟦i:practical knowledge⟧;
⟦b:oppidum⟧, ⟦i:walled town⟧;
⟦b:libellus⟧, ⟦i:little book⟧.
3. Adjectives are not used in immediate agreement with proper names; but an Adjective may limit ⟦b:vir⟧, ⟦b:homō⟧, ⟦b:ille⟧, or some other word used as an Appositive of a proper name; as,—
⟦b:Sōcratēs, homō sapiēns⟧ = ⟦i:the wise Socrates⟧;
⟦b:Scīpiō, vir fortissimus⟧ = ⟦i:the doughty Scipio⟧;
⟦b:Syrācūsae, urbs praeclārissima⟧ = ⟦i:famous Syracuse⟧.
4. An Adjective ⟦i:may be⟧ equivalent to a Possessive or Subjective Genitive; as,—
⟦b:pāstor rēgius⟧, ⟦i:the shepherd of the king⟧;
⟦b:tumultus servīlis⟧, ⟦i:the uprising of the slaves⟧.

1. In case of Defective and Deponent Verbs, a Passive is supplied:—
⟦i:a⟧) By the corresponding verbal Nouns in combination with ⟦b:esse⟧, ⟦i:etc.⟧; as,—
⟦b:in odiō sumus⟧, ⟦i:we are hated⟧;
⟦b:in invidiā sum⟧, ⟦i:I am envied⟧;
⟦b:admīrātiōnī est⟧, ⟦i:he is admired⟧;
⟦b:oblīviōne obruitur⟧, ⟦i:he is forgotten⟧ (lit. ⟦i:is overwhelmed by oblivion⟧);
⟦b:in ūsū esse⟧, ⟦i:to be used⟧.
⟦i:b⟧) By the Passive of Verbs of related meaning. Thus:—
⟦b:agitārī⟧ as Passive of ⟦b:persequī⟧;
⟦b:temptārī⟧ as Passive of ⟦b:adorīrī⟧.
2. The lack of the Perfect Active Participle in Latin is supplied—
⟦i:a⟧) Sometimes by the Perfect Passive Participle of the Deponent; as,—
⟦b:adhortātus⟧, ⟦i:having exhorted⟧;
⟦b:veritus⟧, ⟦i:having feared⟧.
⟦i:b⟧) By the Ablative Absolute; as,—
⟦b:hostium agrīs vāstātīs Caesar exercitum redūxit⟧, ⟦i:having ravaged the country of the enemy, Caesar led back his army⟧.
⟦i:c⟧) By subordinate clauses; as,—
⟦b:eō cum advēnisset, castra posuit⟧, ⟦i:having arrived there, he pitched a camp⟧;
⟦b:hostes quī in urbem irrūperant⟧, ⟦i:the enemy having burst into the city⟧.
3. The Latin agrees with English in the stylistic employment of the Second Person Singular in an indefinite sense (= '⟦i:one⟧'). ⟦i:Cf.⟧ the English '⟦i:You can drive a horse to water, but you can't make him drink.⟧' But in Latin this use is mainly confined to certain varieties of the Subjunctive, especially the Potential (§ 280), Jussive (§ 275), Deliberative (§ 277), and the Subjunctive in conditional sentences of the sort included under § 302, 2, and 303. Examples:—
⟦b:vidērēs⟧, ⟦i:you could see⟧;
⟦b:ūtāre vīribus⟧, ⟦i:use your strength⟧,
⟦b:quid hōc homine faciās⟧, ⟦i:what are you to do with this man⟧?
⟦b:mēns quoque et animus, nisi tamquam lūminī oleum īnstīllēs, exstinguuntur senectūte⟧, ⟦i:the intellect and mind too are extinguished by old age, unless, so to speak, you keep pouring oil into the lamp⟧;
⟦b:tantō amōre possessiōnēs suās amplexī tenēbant, ut ab eīs membra dīvellī citius posse dīcerēs⟧, ⟦i:they clung to their possessions with such an affectionate embrace, that you would have said their limbs could sooner be torn from their bodies⟧.

1. To denote '⟦i:so many years, etc., afterwards or before⟧' the Latin employs not merely the Ablative of Degree of Difference with ⟦b:post⟧ and ⟦b:ante⟧ (see § 223), but has other forms of expression. Thus:—
⟦b:post quīnque annōs⟧, ⟦i:five years afterward⟧;
⟦b:paucōs ante diēs⟧, ⟦i:a few days before⟧;
⟦b:ante quadriennium⟧, ⟦i:four years before⟧;
⟦b:post diem quārtum quam ab urbe discesserāmus⟧, ⟦i:four days after we had left the city⟧;
⟦b:ante tertium annum quam dēcesserat⟧, ⟦i:three years before he had died⟧.
2. The Latin seldom combines both Subject and Object with the same Infinitive; as,—
⟦b:Rōmānōs Hannibalem vīcisse cōnstat⟧.
Such a sentence would be ambiguous, and might mean either that the Romans had conquered Hannibal, or that Hannibal had conquered the Romans. Perspicuity was gained by the use of the Passive Infinitive; as,—
⟦b:Rōmānōs ab Hannibale victōs esse cōnstat⟧, ⟦i:it is well established that the Romans were defeated by Hannibal⟧.
